                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “You rescheduled my—”
   “You booked the first one at five a.m. in the middle of a spiral. I’m
not letting you do the second one in the middle of a war.” She chewed,
swallowed. “It’s your appointment. I just made sure it didn’t die in a
voicemail.”
   I wanted to be angry. It would have been easier. Instead I took the
other sandwich and said, “Fine,” and she said, “Fine,” and we ate in
the cab line in the cold, in a silence we’ve been practicing for two years.



Mamá smelled my hair at the door before she said anything. “Hueles a
ella,” she said. You smell like her.
   Then her eyes went to the backpack on the kitchen chair, and the
rest of her face followed.
   She didn’t ask. She stood very still with the wooden spoon in her
hand and the rice behind her catching, and I watched her go through
about thirty years of arguments in four seconds.
   “¿La trajiste?”
   “Sí.”
   She put the spoon down. “No en mi casa.”
   “Má—”
   “Escúchame.” She held up one hand. “No en mi casa. Not because
I’m afraid of her. Because a man with a basket knows where we live.
They know this building. They know your window.” She crossed her‐
self, quickly, with the hand that had held the spoon. “Put her where
she’s safe. Not in a closet. Where there’s no speaker, and no neighbors
who talk, and a person who knows how to keep a thing.”
   I considered it for about a second.
   “The café.”
   “Ese hombre,” said my mother, “tiene cara de saber guardar cosas.”
   She had met Mr. Rodríguez twice. She’d already decided he had the
face of a man who could keep a secret, a pot, and a grudge.




